// Day 11 question pool: Domain 4, Design Cost-Optimized Architectures. Data only; ids key saved stats, never rename or reuse.
window.QUIZ.push(
{id:"q11-001", day:11, dom:4, tags:["S3 storage classes & lifecycle"],
 q:"A media analytics company stores 800 TB of clickstream exports and video metadata in S3 Standard. Some datasets are queried heavily for a few weeks and then go quiet, while others are rediscovered by data scientists months later; the company cannot predict which datasets will be accessed or when. Every object must stay available with millisecond access and no restore step. Which storage strategy is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Add a lifecycle rule that transitions all objects to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days.",
    "Add a lifecycle rule that transitions all objects to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 90 days.",
    "Store the objects in S3 Intelligent-Tiering and let S3 move each object between access tiers.",
    "Add a lifecycle rule that transitions all objects to S3 One Zone-IA after 30 days."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Standard-IA charges a per-GB retrieval fee on every read. Datasets that become hot again would be read heavily from IA, and with an unpredictable pattern those fees can wipe out the storage savings.",
    "Glacier Flexible Retrieval requires a restore that takes minutes to hours before an object can be read, which breaks the millisecond-access, no-restore requirement.",
    "Correct. Intelligent-Tiering is built for unknown or changing access patterns: it moves objects that go unread to cheaper infrequent and archive instant access tiers and back to the frequent tier when read, with no retrieval fees, only a small per-object monitoring charge.",
    "One Zone-IA keeps data in a single AZ and still charges retrieval fees. This data is not described as re-creatable, so relying on one AZ is not appropriate, and hot datasets would rack up retrieval charges."],
 ref:["d11e2","d11c1","d3c3"], doc:"s3-classes"},

{id:"q11-002", day:11, dom:4, tags:["S3 storage classes & lifecycle"],
 q:"A video-sharing platform keeps every original upload in S3 Standard and generates several lower-resolution renditions of each video. The renditions can be regenerated from the originals by the transcoding pipeline at any time. After 30 days, a rendition is requested only a few times per month, but when it is requested it must be served immediately. Which storage class should a lifecycle rule move renditions to after 30 days to be MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access",
    "S3 Standard-Infrequent Access",
    "S3 Intelligent-Tiering",
    "S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. One Zone-IA gives millisecond access at a lower storage price than Standard-IA by storing data in one AZ. That trade-off is acceptable here because a lost rendition can simply be regenerated from the original.",
    "Standard-IA also gives immediate access, but it costs more per GB because it stores data across multiple AZs, resilience that re-creatable renditions do not need.",
    "Renditions read a few times a month rarely go 30 days unread, so Intelligent-Tiering would keep many in its Frequent Access tier at Standard prices plus a monitoring fee; even its Infrequent Access tier costs as much as Standard-IA, more than One Zone-IA.",
    "Glacier Flexible Retrieval requires a restore that takes minutes to hours, so renditions could not be served immediately when requested."],
 ref:["d11e2","d11c1","d3c3"], doc:"s3-classes"},

{id:"q11-003", day:11, dom:4, tags:["S3 storage classes & lifecycle"],
 q:"A research institute keeps 400 TB of raw instrument data in S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval. Once a year it restores the entire dataset for reprocessing, and the data must be available within 24 hours of starting the restore. The institute wants to minimize the cost of this yearly restore. What should it do?",
 o:["Restore the objects with Expedited retrievals.",
    "Restore the objects with Bulk retrievals.",
    "Restore the objects with Standard retrievals.",
    "Move the objects to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval first."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Expedited retrievals return data in minutes at the highest retrieval price and are meant for small, urgent requests, not 400 TB.",
    "Bulk is the lowest-cost retrieval tier, with no per-GB retrieval charge for Flexible Retrieval, and it typically completes within 5 to 12 hours, well inside the 24-hour window.",
    "Standard retrievals typically finish in 3 to 5 hours but are charged per GB retrieved, which costs more than Bulk when a day is acceptable.",
    "Objects must be restored before they can be copied anyway, and keeping 400 TB in Glacier Instant Retrieval all year would cost far more in storage."],
 ref:["d11e2","d11c1","d3c3"], doc:"s3-classes"},

{id:"q11-004", day:11, dom:4, tags:["S3 storage classes & lifecycle"],
 q:"A brokerage must retain trade confirmation records for 10 years to satisfy regulators. The records are written once and are almost never read. When an auditor requests specific records, the firm has five business days to produce them. The firm wants the lowest possible storage cost for these records in Amazon S3. Which approach is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Upload the records to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval and use bulk retrievals for audits.",
    "Upload the records to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval so they can be produced immediately.",
    "Upload the records to S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the Deep Archive Access tier activated.",
    "Upload the records directly to the S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Flexible Retrieval meets the five-day window easily, but its storage price is higher than Deep Archive, and nothing in the requirements needs its faster restores.",
    "Instant Retrieval charges much more per GB than Deep Archive for millisecond access that a five-day audit window doesn't need.",
    "Intelligent-Tiering adds a per-object monitoring fee and only moves an object to Deep Archive Access after at least 180 days without access, so each record pays higher-tier prices for months first. Writing straight to Deep Archive is cheaper for data known to be cold.",
    "Correct. Deep Archive is the lowest-cost S3 storage class for long-term retention of rarely accessed data. Standard restores complete within 12 hours and bulk restores within 48 hours, well inside the five-day audit window."],
 ref:["d11e2","d11c1","d3c3"], doc:"s3-classes"},

{id:"q11-005", day:11, dom:4, tags:["S3 storage classes & lifecycle"],
 q:"A data engineering team writes intermediate Parquet files to Amazon S3 during a nightly pipeline. Downstream jobs read each file two or three times during the next week, and the files are never needed after 10 days. A team member proposes writing the files to S3 Standard-IA instead of S3 Standard to reduce the storage bill. Which approach is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Write the files to S3 Standard-IA and add a lifecycle rule that expires them after 10 days.",
    "Write the files to S3 Standard and add a lifecycle rule that expires them after 10 days.",
    "Write the files to S3 One Zone-IA and add a lifecycle rule that expires them after 10 days.",
    "Write the files to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval and add a lifecycle rule that expires them after 10 days."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Standard-IA bills a minimum of 30 days of storage per object and charges per-GB retrieval fees. Files deleted after 10 days and read several times would cost more than in S3 Standard.",
    "Correct. For short-lived, actively read data, S3 Standard has no minimum storage duration and no retrieval fees. An expiration rule deletes the files automatically after 10 days so nobody pays for forgotten intermediates.",
    "One Zone-IA has the same 30-day minimum storage charge and per-GB retrieval fees as Standard-IA, so 10-day files that are read several times would still cost more, with less resilience.",
    "Glacier Instant Retrieval has a 90-day minimum storage charge and higher retrieval fees, which makes it the most expensive choice for files that live 10 days and are read repeatedly."],
 ref:["d11e2","d3c3"], doc:"s3-classes"},

{id:"q11-006", day:11, dom:4, tags:["S3 storage classes & lifecycle","S3 features"],
 q:"An e-commerce company stores product images in a versioned S3 bucket that has grown far faster than its product catalog, and the storefront reads the current images constantly. An S3 Storage Lens dashboard shows that most of the bucket's bytes are noncurrent object versions and that a large amount of storage is held by incomplete multipart uploads. The company must be able to recover an overwritten or deleted image for 30 days. Which actions will reduce storage costs while meeting this requirement? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Suspend versioning on the bucket so that overwrites no longer create new versions.",
    "Add a lifecycle rule that permanently deletes noncurrent versions 30 days after they become noncurrent.",
    "Add a lifecycle rule that expires current object versions 30 days after they are created.",
    "Add a lifecycle rule that transitions current object versions to S3 One Zone-IA after 30 days.",
    "Add a lifecycle rule that aborts incomplete multipart uploads 7 days after they are started."],
 a:[1,4],
 x:["Suspending versioning doesn't remove the noncurrent versions already stored, so they keep being billed, and future overwrites could no longer be recovered, which breaks the 30-day requirement.",
    "Correct. A noncurrent version expiration action keeps every previous version for the required 30 days and then deletes it, removing the bulk of the stored bytes automatically.",
    "Expiring current versions puts delete markers on live product images the storefront still needs, and the hidden versions keep being billed, so it breaks the site without cutting the waste.",
    "Current images are the ones the storefront reads, so One Zone-IA would add retrieval fees and reduce resilience, and it does nothing about the noncurrent versions and upload parts that make up most of the bytes.",
    "Correct. Parts of multipart uploads that were never completed don't appear in normal object listings but are billed as storage. An abort-incomplete-multipart-upload rule cleans them up automatically."],
 ref:["d11e2"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q11-007", day:11, dom:4, tags:["S3 features","Cost management tools"],
 q:"A company has 300 AWS accounts in AWS Organizations and stores data in thousands of S3 buckets across several Regions. The FinOps team wants a single view to find buckets without lifecycle rules, buckets holding large amounts of rarely read data, and accounts where incomplete multipart uploads are growing. The team also wants built-in cost-optimization recommendations. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Configure S3 Inventory on every bucket and query the inventory reports with Amazon Athena.",
    "Use AWS Cost Explorer grouped by linked account and S3 usage type to locate the buckets.",
    "Create an organization-wide S3 Storage Lens dashboard with advanced metrics turned on.",
    "Enable CloudTrail data events for all buckets and analyze the S3 request logs with Athena."],
 a:[2],
 x:["S3 Inventory lists objects per bucket, but configuring it across thousands of buckets in 300 accounts and building queries to derive lifecycle, activity, and multipart upload insights is a lot of ongoing work.",
    "Cost Explorer shows what S3 costs by account and usage type, but not which buckets lack lifecycle rules, how often data is read, or where incomplete multipart uploads accumulate.",
    "Correct. Storage Lens aggregates metrics for all accounts and Regions in the organization into one dashboard, including incomplete multipart upload bytes; advanced metrics add activity metrics to spot cold buckets, lifecycle rule counts, and contextual recommendations.",
    "CloudTrail data events are billed per event and would be costly at this scale; they also say nothing about lifecycle configuration or multipart upload storage, so the team would have to build its own analysis."],
 ref:["d11e2"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q11-008", day:11, dom:4, tags:["EBS & instance store"],
 q:"A SaaS company runs 400 EC2 instances, each with a 1 TiB gp2 data volume that was sized partly to get enough baseline IOPS. CloudWatch shows each volume peaks at about 2,500 IOPS and 100 MiB/s of throughput. The company wants to reduce EBS costs without downtime and without lowering performance. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Use Elastic Volumes to change each volume to io2 with 3,000 provisioned IOPS.",
    "Snapshot each volume and restore it as a smaller 300 GiB gp2 volume.",
    "Use Elastic Volumes to change each volume to st1 Throughput Optimized HDD.",
    "Use Elastic Volumes to change each volume to gp3 at its baseline performance."],
 a:[3],
 x:["io2 is built for sustained high IOPS and low-latency mission-critical databases; it costs more per GB plus a per-IOPS charge, so it raises costs for a load that gp3 covers at baseline.",
    "A 300 GiB gp2 volume has a baseline of only 900 IOPS (3 IOPS per GiB), so sustained performance would drop, and a snapshot-and-restore swap needs downtime for each instance.",
    "st1 is an HDD type optimized for large sequential throughput; it cannot deliver about 2,500 small random IOPS, so application performance would fall.",
    "Correct. gp3 costs less per GB than gp2 and includes 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s at any size, which covers this workload. Elastic Volumes changes the type in place while the volume stays attached and in use."],
 ref:["d11e2","d3c1"], doc:"ebs"},

{id:"q11-009", day:11, dom:4, tags:["EBS & instance store"],
 q:"After a year of frequent blue/green deployments, a company finds hundreds of unattached EBS volumes left behind by terminated instances, and tens of thousands of manually created snapshots, far more than its 14-day backup policy requires. The unattached volumes hold no data that needs to be kept. The company wants to cut these costs now and stop the problem from coming back with the LEAST ongoing effort. Which actions should a solutions architect take? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Delete the unattached volumes and set DeleteOnTermination to true for data volumes in the launch templates.",
    "Change the unattached volumes to sc1 Cold HDD so that they cost less per GB while they are kept.",
    "Delete the old manual snapshots and use Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager policies that keep 14 days of snapshots.",
    "Move all existing snapshots to the EBS Snapshots Archive tier to lower their monthly storage price.",
    "Attach the unattached volumes to a utility instance so that they are visible and included in backups."],
 a:[0,2],
 x:["Correct. Unattached volumes are billed for their full provisioned size whether used or not. Deleting them stops the charge, and DeleteOnTermination makes future data volumes go away with their instances.",
    "sc1 lowers the price per GB, but the company would still pay every month for volumes that hold nothing of value; deleting them removes the cost entirely.",
    "Correct. Data Lifecycle Manager creates snapshots on a schedule and automatically deletes those older than the retention rule, replacing manual snapshots that nobody cleans up.",
    "The archive tier is meant for rarely accessed snapshots kept 90 days or longer; it still bills for snapshots the 14-day policy says are not needed, and it does nothing to stop new ones piling up.",
    "Attaching useless volumes to an instance keeps paying for them and adds instance and backup costs, the opposite of the goal."],
 ref:["d11e2","d3c1"], doc:"ebs"},

{id:"q11-010", day:11, dom:4, tags:["EFS"],
 q:"A research institute stores 60 TB of genomics results on an Amazon EFS file system (Regional, Standard storage class) mounted by analysis instances in three AZs. Files are analyzed heavily for about two weeks and then rarely opened, but researchers sometimes reopen old files through the same mount path. The data must stay resilient to the loss of an AZ. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Use AWS DataSync to move files older than 14 days to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval and delete them from EFS.",
    "Migrate the data to a new EFS One Zone file system in one of the three AZs.",
    "Enable EFS lifecycle management to move files to Infrequent Access after 14 days without access.",
    "Migrate the data to an Amazon FSx for Lustre scratch file system linked to an S3 bucket."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Old files would no longer be at the same mount path and would need a restore taking minutes to hours, which breaks how researchers reopen them.",
    "A One Zone file system keeps data in a single AZ, which fails the requirement to survive the loss of an AZ.",
    "Correct. Lifecycle management moves files not accessed for 14 days to the lower-cost Infrequent Access storage class while they stay in the same file system and path, and can move a file back to Standard when it is read again.",
    "Scratch file systems are for temporary high-performance processing; they do not replicate data and are not meant for durable long-term storage, and the migration adds work."],
 ref:["d11e2","d3c1"], doc:"efs"},

{id:"q11-011", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Storage Gateway"],
 q:"A design firm's on-premises NAS holds 400 TB of project files and is nearly full. Designers work only with files from active projects, about 10 TB at a time, and need LAN-speed SMB access to them from Windows workstations. The firm wants to stop buying storage hardware and use lifecycle rules to move completed projects to lower-cost storage. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Deploy AWS Storage Gateway Volume Gateway in stored mode and back up the volumes to Amazon S3.",
    "Deploy Amazon S3 File Gateway on premises and store the project files as objects in Amazon S3.",
    "Migrate the files to Amazon FSx for Windows File Server and access the share over Site-to-Site VPN.",
    "Buy additional NAS shelves and use AWS DataSync to copy the NAS nightly to S3 Glacier Deep Archive."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Stored volumes keep the complete dataset on premises and only back it up asynchronously to AWS, so the firm would still have to buy local capacity for all 400 TB.",
    "Correct. S3 File Gateway presents an SMB share backed by S3 and caches recently used files locally for LAN-speed access. The full dataset lives in S3, where lifecycle rules can move completed projects to colder storage classes.",
    "Every file open would cross the WAN, so designers lose LAN-speed access, and keeping all 400 TB on FSx file storage costs more than S3 with lifecycle tiering.",
    "This keeps buying storage hardware, which the firm wants to stop, and pays for a second copy of all the data in AWS."],
 ref:["d11e2"], doc:"storagegateway"},

{id:"q11-012", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Migration & transfer"],
 q:"A biotech company must move 80 TB of instrument data from an on-premises NFS share to Amazon S3 over its existing 10 Gbps AWS Direct Connect connection, which has plenty of spare capacity. After the initial copy, new and changed files must be copied every night, with integrity verification, until the lab closes in 6 months. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Deploy an AWS DataSync agent on premises and run a scheduled DataSync task to the S3 bucket.",
    "Deploy Amazon S3 File Gateway on premises and script nightly copies from the NFS share onto it.",
    "Run a cron job on an on-premises server that calls <code>aws s3 sync</code> every night.",
    "Create an AWS Transfer Family SFTP server and push the files from the lab with SFTP clients."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. DataSync handles NFS-to-S3 transfers end to end: it copies only new and changed files, verifies data integrity, retries failures, and runs on a schedule, and the spare 10 Gbps capacity can carry the initial 80 TB within days.",
    "File Gateway is built for ongoing hybrid file access, not migration; the team would have to script the copies and change detection, size a local cache for the upload backlog, and build its own verification.",
    "This can work, but the team must build and maintain the scheduling, retries, monitoring, and integrity checks that DataSync provides as a managed service.",
    "Transfer Family is for partners exchanging files over SFTP or FTPS; it bills for each enabled protocol endpoint and for the data transferred, and the lab would still need to script uploads, change detection, and verification."],
 ref:["d11e2"], doc:"datasync"},

{id:"q11-013", day:11, dom:4, tags:["EC2 purchase options"],
 q:"A logistics company has a steady hourly compute spend. Today it runs m6i and c6i instances in two Regions, but over the next 3 years it plans to move most services to AWS Fargate and AWS Lambda and to adopt Graviton instance types for the rest. The company wants a discount in exchange for committing to its baseline spend, without limiting these changes. Which purchase option is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["EC2 Instance Savings Plans for the m6i and c6i families in each Region",
    "Convertible Reserved Instances that can be exchanged for other instance types",
    "Compute Savings Plans covering the baseline hourly compute spend",
    "Standard Reserved Instances for the current instance types in each Region"],
 a:[2],
 x:["EC2 Instance Savings Plans are tied to one instance family in one Region and do not apply to Fargate or Lambda, so the discount would be lost as services move.",
    "Convertible RIs can be exchanged among EC2 instance configurations, but they never apply to Fargate or Lambda usage, which is where most of the spend is heading.",
    "Correct. Compute Savings Plans apply automatically to EC2 usage in any family, size, Region, or OS, and to Fargate and Lambda, so the commitment keeps earning a discount through the planned changes.",
    "Standard RIs are locked to specific instance attributes and cover only EC2, so they would go unused after the move to Graviton, Fargate, and Lambda."],
 ref:["d11e3","d11c2","d1c7"], doc:"savings-plans"},

{id:"q11-014", day:11, dom:4, tags:["EC2 purchase options"],
 q:"A gaming company runs its matchmaking fleet on r7i instances in one Region 24/7, and the service must not be interrupted. The fleet will stay on the r7i instance family in that Region for at least 3 years, but the team expects to change instance sizes as player numbers grow and to move some instances from Windows to Linux. Which purchase option gives the LARGEST discount while allowing these changes?",
 o:["A Compute Savings Plan for the fleet's steady hourly spend",
    "An EC2 Instance Savings Plan for the r7i family in that Region",
    "Standard Reserved Instances for the current Windows instance sizes",
    "Spot Instances requested through a capacity-optimized EC2 Fleet"],
 a:[1],
 x:["A Compute Savings Plan would also cover the changes, but it trades a smaller discount for flexibility across families and Regions that this company does not need.",
    "Correct. An EC2 Instance Savings Plan gives the deepest Savings Plans discount in exchange for committing to one instance family in one Region, and it still applies across sizes, operating systems, and tenancy within that family.",
    "Standard RIs are bought for a specific platform: Windows reservations would stop applying to instances moved to Linux, and Windows RIs don't get instance size flexibility.",
    "Spot Instances can be interrupted with a two-minute warning, which the always-on matchmaking service cannot tolerate."],
 ref:["d11e3","d11c2","d1c7"], doc:"savings-plans"},

{id:"q11-015", day:11, dom:4, tags:["EC2 purchase options"],
 q:"A visual effects studio renders frames every night on about 500 instances. Each frame is an independent task that checkpoints its progress, and failed tasks are retried automatically; the full job must finish by morning, but its exact start time is flexible. The studio currently uses On-Demand c6i.4xlarge instances in a single AZ. Which approach is MOST cost-effective while keeping the risk of losing capacity low?",
 o:["Request Spot Instances of only c6i.4xlarge in the current AZ with the lowest-price allocation strategy.",
    "Purchase 3-year Standard Reserved Instances for 500 c6i.4xlarge instances in the current AZ.",
    "Keep the On-Demand Instances and cover their usage with a 1-year Compute Savings Plan.",
    "Use Spot Instances across many instance types and all AZs with the price-capacity-optimized strategy."],
 a:[3],
 x:["A single instance type in one AZ is one Spot capacity pool, so a capacity crunch there can interrupt or block most of the fleet at once and risk the morning deadline.",
    "Reserved Instances bill for every hour of the term, but the fleet runs only overnight, so most of the reserved hours would be paid for and unused.",
    "A Savings Plan commitment is billed every hour, so the daytime hours when nothing runs would be wasted, and its discount is much smaller than typical Spot savings.",
    "Correct. Interruptible, checkpointed batch work is ideal for Spot. Diversifying across instance types and AZs with price-capacity-optimized places requests in deep, low-priced pools, lowering both cost and interruption risk."],
 ref:["d11e3","d11c2","d1c7"], doc:"ec2-pricing"},

{id:"q11-016", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Auto Scaling","EC2 purchase options"],
 q:"A news website runs a stateless web tier in an EC2 Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer. At least 4 instances must always be running to serve baseline traffic, and breaking-news spikes can require up to 40 instances for a few hours. Any instance can be replaced at any time without affecting users. Which configuration choices are MOST cost-effective while keeping the baseline reliable? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Set the On-Demand base capacity to 0 and run 100% of the group on Spot Instances.",
    "Purchase Standard Reserved Instances for 40 instances so the peak is always discounted.",
    "Use a mixed instances policy with an On-Demand base of 4 and Spot for all capacity above it.",
    "Use a single instance type with the lowest-price Spot allocation strategy in one AZ.",
    "List several instance types and sizes and use the price-capacity-optimized allocation strategy."],
 a:[2,4],
 x:["With no On-Demand base, a wave of Spot interruptions could take out the instances serving baseline traffic, which must always be available.",
    "The group needs 40 instances only during short spikes, so most of those reservations would sit unused while still being billed every hour.",
    "Correct. The On-Demand base guarantees the 4 instances that must always run, while the bursty capacity above it uses Spot, which suits a stateless tier where instances can be replaced at any time.",
    "One instance type in one AZ is a single Spot pool, which raises interruption risk and removes the AZ redundancy a web tier needs.",
    "Correct. Diversifying instance types lets the group draw from many Spot pools, and price-capacity-optimized chooses pools that are both low-priced and least likely to be interrupted."],
 ref:["d11e3","d5c2"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q11-017", day:11, dom:4, tags:["EC2 instance types","Cost management tools"],
 q:"A company runs about 1,200 EC2 instances in Auto Scaling groups, plus thousands of EBS volumes and Lambda functions, across 20 accounts in AWS Organizations. Finance suspects much of it is overprovisioned. The company wants ready-made right-sizing recommendations for instances, Auto Scaling groups, EBS volumes, and Lambda memory settings that are based on measured utilization, including memory utilization. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Opt in to AWS Compute Optimizer for the organization and publish memory metrics with the CloudWatch agent.",
    "Turn on the rightsizing recommendations in AWS Cost Explorer for all linked accounts in the organization.",
    "Build CloudWatch dashboards of CPU and memory metrics and have engineers review them every month.",
    "Create AWS Budgets usage budgets for each instance type and Lambda function to flag overspending."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. Compute Optimizer analyzes each resource's configuration and CloudWatch utilization metrics and recommends sizes for EC2 instances, Auto Scaling groups, EBS volumes, and Lambda memory across the organization; the CloudWatch agent supplies the memory metrics.",
    "Cost Explorer rightsizing recommendations cover EC2 instances only, so the EBS volumes and Lambda functions would be left out.",
    "Manual dashboard reviews across 20 accounts are labor-intensive and produce no ready-made size recommendations.",
    "Budgets alert when spend or usage crosses a threshold; they don't analyze utilization or recommend smaller resources."],
 ref:["d11e3"], doc:"compute-optimizer"},

{id:"q11-018", day:11, dom:4, tags:["EC2 instance types","Containers"],
 q:"A fintech startup runs Go and Java microservices in containers on Amazon ECS with an EC2 capacity provider of m6i instances. The services have no x86-specific dependencies, and the CI pipeline can already build multi-architecture images. The company wants to cut compute cost for the same performance with minimal application changes. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Move the services to t3 burstable instances in unlimited mode.",
    "Move the services to Graviton-based m7g instances with arm64 images.",
    "Move the services to m6i Dedicated Instances for steadier performance.",
    "Consolidate the services onto fewer, larger m6i instance sizes."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Steady microservice load would keep burstable instances above their CPU baseline, so unlimited mode adds surplus-credit charges, and performance would not match.",
    "Correct. Graviton instances offer better price performance than comparable x86 instances, and Go and Java run natively on arm64, so only the image architecture changes.",
    "Dedicated Instances run on hardware reserved for one customer and cost more; they do not improve price performance.",
    "Within an instance family, price scales linearly with size, so fewer larger m6i instances cost about the same for the same total capacity and do not improve price performance."],
 ref:["d11e3"], doc:"ec2"},

{id:"q11-019", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Lambda"],
 q:"A real-estate website lets agents upload listing photos to an S3 bucket. Each photo must be resized into three sizes, which takes about 5 seconds per photo. Uploads arrive in bursts during business hours, ranging from zero to several hundred per minute, and almost none arrive at night. Which compute option is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["An EC2 instance that runs 24/7 and polls the bucket for new photos",
    "An ECS service on Fargate with two tasks that always run and read an SQS queue",
    "An EC2 Auto Scaling group with a minimum of one instance that scales on queue depth",
    "A Lambda function invoked by S3 event notifications for each upload"],
 a:[3],
 x:["A single always-on instance is paid for all night when there is no work, and it could fall behind during bursts of several hundred uploads a minute.",
    "Two tasks that always run are billed around the clock, including the idle nights, and would still need to scale out for bursts.",
    "The minimum instance is billed even when idle, and new instances take minutes to launch, so bursts queue up; there is also more to manage.",
    "Correct. Lambda scales with each upload burst and bills per request and per millisecond of run time, so short, spiky, event-driven work costs nothing while idle."],
 ref:["d11e3","d1c8"], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"q11-020", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Containers"],
 q:"A pharmaceutical company runs a containerized molecular simulation that takes 1–3 hours and needs 4 vCPUs and 16 GB of memory. Scientists start it 4–6 times a day at unpredictable times, and there is no other work for the compute between runs. The team does not want to manage servers or clusters. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Run each simulation as an Amazon ECS task on AWS Fargate when a scientist submits it.",
    "Run each simulation in an AWS Lambda function configured with 10 GB of memory.",
    "Run the simulations on an m6i.xlarge EC2 instance that stays on 24/7 with a job queue.",
    "Run the simulations on an Amazon EKS cluster with a managed node group of one m6i.xlarge."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. Fargate bills per second for the vCPU and memory a task uses, only while it runs, with no servers to manage, which fits long but sporadic container jobs.",
    "A Lambda invocation can run for at most 15 minutes (90 minutes for asynchronous invocations on Lambda Managed Instances), so a simulation that takes up to 3 hours cannot run there.",
    "The instance would be paid for during the many idle hours between runs, overlapping runs would have to wait, and the team would have to patch and manage it.",
    "EKS adds a per-cluster charge and a node that is billed while idle, plus the cluster management the team wants to avoid."],
 ref:["d11e3","d1c8"], doc:"fargate"},

{id:"q11-021", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Auto Scaling"],
 q:"A software company runs 150 development EC2 instances and 30 Auto Scaling groups for test environments. Engineers use them only from 8 AM to 7 PM on weekdays, but everything runs 24/7. The development instances are stateful servers whose EBS data must be kept between sessions, while the test groups can be rebuilt from launch templates at any time. Which actions are MOST cost-effective? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Purchase 1-year Standard Reserved Instances to discount all development and test instances.",
    "Use Instance Scheduler on AWS to stop the development instances outside working hours.",
    "Run the development servers as Spot Instances so that AWS reclaims their idle capacity overnight.",
    "Add scheduled actions that scale the test groups to zero each evening and back each morning.",
    "Terminate the development instances every evening and launch fresh ones from the AMI each morning."],
 a:[1,3],
 x:["Reservations bill for all 168 hours a week, while the instances are needed for only about 55 of them; stopping them outside working hours saves more than any RI discount.",
    "Correct. Stopped instances incur no instance charges, only EBS storage, and their volumes keep the data. Instance Scheduler automates the daily stop and start on a defined schedule.",
    "Spot doesn't stop billing at night just because a server is idle, and interruptions during the working day would disrupt engineers' stateful servers.",
    "Correct. Scheduled scaling actions bring the test environments to zero instances overnight and at weekends and restore them each morning, with no custom code.",
    "Terminating instances deletes root volumes by default and loses work on the stateful servers, which breaks the requirement to keep EBS data."],
 ref:["d11e3","d5c2"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q11-022", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Containers","EC2 purchase options"],
 q:"A company runs a stateless image-thumbnail service as an Amazon ECS service on AWS Fargate with 40 tasks around the clock. The tasks pull work from an SQS queue, so if a task is stopped, its message returns to the queue and another task processes it. The company wants to lower compute cost significantly while always keeping at least 10 tasks on capacity that cannot be interrupted. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Move the service to EC2 On-Demand Instances in an Auto Scaling group that an ECS capacity provider manages.",
    "Purchase an EC2 Instance Savings Plan for the instance family that Fargate uses to run the service's tasks.",
    "Use a capacity provider strategy with a FARGATE base of 10 tasks and the remaining tasks on FARGATE_SPOT.",
    "Shrink every task to the smallest Fargate size and keep running all 40 tasks on regular Fargate capacity."],
 a:[2],
 x:["On-Demand EC2 adds instances to manage and patch and does not offer anything close to the Spot discount.",
    "EC2 Instance Savings Plans apply only to EC2 instances, not to Fargate; Fargate usage can be covered only by Compute Savings Plans.",
    "Fargate Spot runs interruption-tolerant tasks at a steep discount, and the FARGATE base keeps 10 tasks on regular capacity, so the minimum is never interrupted.",
    "Shrinking tasks without measuring their needs risks failures, and regular Fargate pricing saves far less than Fargate Spot."],
 ref:["d11e3","d11c2"], doc:"fargate"},

{id:"q11-023", day:11, dom:4, tags:["EC2 instance types"],
 q:"A company runs an internal wiki and ticketing application on m6i.large instances (2 vCPUs, 8 GiB). CloudWatch shows average CPU below 10%, with brief spikes to about 60% for a few minutes when reports run a few times a day. Memory use is steady at about 3 GiB. Which instance type is MOST cost-effective while still handling the spikes?",
 o:["t3.medium burstable instances (2 vCPUs, 4 GiB)",
    "c6i.large compute optimized instances (2 vCPUs, 4 GiB)",
    "m6i.xlarge general purpose instances (4 vCPUs, 16 GiB)",
    "r6i.large memory optimized instances (2 vCPUs, 16 GiB)"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. Burstable instances suit low average CPU with short spikes: they earn CPU credits while idle and spend them during the report runs, at a much lower hourly price, and 4 GiB covers the 3 GiB memory need.",
    "Compute optimized instances price in sustained high CPU performance that this mostly idle workload doesn't use, so they cost more than a burstable instance.",
    "Doubling the size roughly doubles the price for capacity the application never uses.",
    "Memory optimized instances add memory the application doesn't need, at a higher price than its current instances."],
 ref:["d11e3"], doc:"ec2"},

{id:"q11-024", day:11, dom:4, tags:["EC2 purchase options","Analytics"],
 q:"A retail analytics team runs a 4-hour Apache Spark job on an Amazon EMR cluster every night. The cluster has a primary node, core nodes that store HDFS data, and task nodes that only provide compute; Spark retries failed tasks automatically. The job must complete reliably every night. Which choices are MOST cost-effective? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Run the primary node and the core nodes on On-Demand Instances.",
    "Run the primary node on Spot Instances to save the most on the cluster.",
    "Run the core nodes on Spot Instances so that HDFS capacity costs less.",
    "Run the task nodes on Spot Instances using instance fleets with several instance types.",
    "Buy 3-year Standard Reserved Instances for every node and keep the cluster running."],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["Correct. Losing the primary node ends the cluster, and losing core nodes loses HDFS data, so keeping them On-Demand protects the nightly run.",
    "If a Spot primary node is reclaimed, the entire cluster terminates and that night's job fails.",
    "Interrupted core nodes take their HDFS blocks with them, which can fail or stall the job and break the reliability requirement.",
    "Correct. Task nodes hold no HDFS data, so an interruption only makes Spark retry tasks; diversified instance fleets make Spot capacity easier to obtain at low cost.",
    "The job runs 4 hours a night, so a cluster kept running on reservations pays for about 20 idle hours every day."],
 ref:["d11e3","d11c2"], doc:"emr"},

{id:"q11-025", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Cost management tools","EC2 purchase options"],
 q:"A media company's EC2, Fargate, and Lambda usage has been stable for 6 months. Before buying a Savings Plan, the finance team wants a recommended hourly commitment based on the company's actual usage history, with estimated savings and the expected utilization of that commitment for 1-year and 3-year terms. Which AWS tool provides this with the LEAST effort?",
 o:["AWS Compute Optimizer recommendations for each resource type",
    "AWS Pricing Calculator estimates built from the current inventory",
    "Savings Plans purchase recommendations in AWS Cost Explorer",
    "An AWS Budgets Savings Plans coverage budget for the account"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Compute Optimizer recommends instance sizes and Lambda memory settings; it does not calculate a Savings Plans commitment.",
    "The Pricing Calculator estimates costs from inputs you type in, not from the account's actual hourly usage history, so modeling a commitment this way is manual.",
    "Correct. Cost Explorer analyzes historical usage and recommends a Savings Plans hourly commitment for the chosen plan type, term, and payment option, with estimated monthly savings and the estimated utilization of the commitment.",
    "Coverage budgets track how much usage existing Savings Plans cover and alert when it drops below a target; they don't recommend what to buy."],
 ref:["d11e3","d11c2"], doc:"costexplorer"},

{id:"q11-026", day:11, dom:4, tags:["RDS"],
 q:"A healthcare company runs a production Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL Multi-AZ DB instance (db.r6g.2xlarge) 24/7. Its load has been stable for two years and is expected to stay on the same instance class for the next three years. The company wants the lowest cost for this database without reducing availability. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Purchase a Compute Savings Plan that covers the database's hourly cost.",
    "Convert the database to Single-AZ and purchase a reservation for it.",
    "Migrate the database to an Aurora PostgreSQL serverless cluster to pay per use.",
    "Purchase a 3-year Reserved DB instance for the Multi-AZ configuration."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Compute Savings Plans apply only to EC2, Fargate, and Lambda usage; RDS DB instances are discounted by Reserved DB instances or the separate Database Savings Plans.",
    "Dropping Multi-AZ removes the standby, which reduces availability and breaks the stated requirement.",
    "Aurora serverless (formerly Aurora Serverless v2) pays off for variable or intermittent load; a steady 24/7 load costs more in ACU-hours than a reserved provisioned instance, and the migration adds work.",
    "Correct. A Reserved DB instance bought for the Multi-AZ deployment keeps the standby, and a 3-year term gives the deepest discount for a steady database, more than a Database Savings Plan, which is 1-year only and discounts provisioned instances less."],
 ref:["d11e4"], doc:"rds"},

{id:"q11-027", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Aurora"],
 q:"A startup has six Aurora MySQL development and test clusters, each running a provisioned db.r6g.large writer 24/7. Developers use a cluster for a few hours on some days, automated test runs cause short bursts of heavy load, and there are long idle periods that are hard to predict. The startup wants to minimize cost without anyone having to start, stop, or resize clusters manually. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Purchase 1-year Reserved DB instances for the six db.r6g.large writers.",
    "Convert the clusters to Aurora serverless with a minimum capacity of 0 ACUs.",
    "Configure Aurora Auto Scaling to add Aurora Replicas during the test bursts.",
    "Downsize the writers to db.t4g.medium burstable instances that run 24/7."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Reservations discount the hourly rate but still pay for every idle night and weekend on clusters that are used only a few hours at a time.",
    "Correct. Aurora serverless (formerly Aurora Serverless v2) scales capacity up for test bursts and down when quiet, and with a minimum of 0 ACUs an idle cluster pauses automatically, so compute is billed only while it is used (storage is billed as usual).",
    "Aurora Auto Scaling adds read replicas, which adds instance cost; the writer still runs 24/7, and replicas don't help write-heavy test loads.",
    "Burstable writers still bill around the clock, and heavy test bursts would drain CPU credits and slow the tests down."],
 ref:["d11e4"], doc:"aurora"},

{id:"q11-028", day:11, dom:4, tags:["RDS"],
 q:"A finance department uses an Amazon RDS for MySQL DB instance only during the last three days of each quarter to run reconciliation reports. The rest of the time the instance is idle, but its data must be kept for the next quarter. Which approach is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Take a snapshot after each quarter's reports, delete the instance, and restore from the snapshot next quarter.",
    "Stop the DB instance after each quarter's reports and start it again at the end of the next quarter.",
    "Purchase a 1-year Reserved DB instance so that the instance's hourly rate is discounted all year.",
    "Modify the instance to the smallest available instance class between quarters and scale up when needed."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. Between quarters only snapshot storage is billed, with no instance-hours or provisioned storage, and restoring from the snapshot brings the data back when the reports are due.",
    "RDS automatically starts a stopped DB instance after 7 days, so it would be running and billed for most of the quarter, and provisioned storage is billed even while stopped.",
    "A reservation bills for every hour of the year although the database is needed for only about 12 days of it.",
    "The instance would still bill every hour plus its provisioned storage, and each resize means a brief outage and manual work."],
 ref:["d11e4"], doc:"rds"},

{id:"q11-029", day:11, dom:4, tags:["DynamoDB"],
 q:"A startup is launching a mobile game backend on Amazon DynamoDB. It has no traffic history; usage is expected to be close to zero for long stretches, with sudden, unpredictable spikes whenever influencers post about the game. The team wants to avoid throttling and pay only for the requests it serves, without any capacity planning. Which configuration is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Provisioned capacity set to handle the largest spike the team expects",
    "Provisioned capacity with auto scaling plus reserved capacity for the base",
    "On-demand capacity mode for the table and its global secondary indexes",
    "Low provisioned capacity with a DAX cluster in front to absorb the spikes"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Capacity provisioned for the peak is billed every hour, so the long near-idle stretches would be paid for at spike levels.",
    "Auto scaling adjusts over minutes, so sudden spikes can throttle before capacity catches up, and reserved capacity is a long-term commitment the team cannot size without traffic history.",
    "Correct. On-demand mode bills per request, needs no capacity planning, and instantly accommodates up to double the table's previous peak (new tables start at thousands of requests per second), while idle periods cost nothing for throughput.",
    "DAX is a cache that offloads repeated reads, so write spikes would still throttle a table with low provisioned capacity, and the DAX nodes are billed every hour even while the game is idle."],
 ref:["d11e4"], doc:"dynamodb"},

{id:"q11-030", day:11, dom:4, tags:["DynamoDB"],
 q:"An IoT company's write-heavy DynamoDB table has served a mature, highly predictable workload for 2 years: traffic follows a smooth daily curve on top of a steady baseline that runs around the clock. The table uses on-demand capacity mode, and throughput charges dominate its cost. Which actions will reduce cost the MOST while avoiding throttling? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Change the table class to DynamoDB Standard-Infrequent Access.",
    "Switch the table to provisioned capacity mode with auto scaling.",
    "Purchase DynamoDB reserved capacity to cover the steady baseline.",
    "Add a DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) cluster in front of the table.",
    "Convert the table to a global table with a replica in another Region."],
 a:[1,2],
 x:["Standard-IA lowers the storage price but raises throughput prices, so it suits storage-dominated tables, not this throughput-dominated one.",
    "Correct. For steady, predictable traffic, provisioned capacity costs less per request than on-demand, and auto scaling follows the smooth daily curve to avoid throttling.",
    "Correct. Reserved capacity gives an additional discount on provisioned throughput committed for 1 year (3 years in some Regions), which fits a baseline that has been stable for 2 years.",
    "DAX caches reads, but this workload is write-heavy, so DAX would add node costs without reducing most of the throughput spend.",
    "Global tables replicate every write to another Region, adding replicated write and data transfer charges instead of reducing cost."],
 ref:["d11e4"], doc:"dynamodb"},

{id:"q11-031", day:11, dom:4, tags:["DynamoDB"],
 q:"A ride-hailing app stores GPS location pings in a DynamoDB table. Each ping is needed for 30 days to resolve trip disputes and then has no value. The table has grown to many terabytes, and a nightly Lambda function that scans for old items and deletes them consumes large amounts of read and write capacity. The company wants the MOST cost-effective way to remove old data with minimal application changes. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Change the table class to DynamoDB Standard-IA to lower the cost of storing old pings.",
    "Add a global secondary index on the timestamp and change the function to query it.",
    "Export the table to S3 each month, then delete and recreate the table with no data.",
    "Enable Time to Live on an expiry timestamp attribute and retire the cleanup function."],
 a:[3],
 x:["This lowers the storage price but keeps paying to store data that has no value, and the costly nightly scan-and-delete continues.",
    "Querying an index avoids the full scan, but every delete still consumes write capacity, and the index adds its own storage and write costs.",
    "Recreating the table each month throws away pings that are still inside their 30-day dispute window and adds downtime and operational risk.",
    "Correct. With TTL, the app writes an expiry time on each item and DynamoDB deletes expired items in the background without consuming write throughput, so the scan-and-delete function is no longer needed."],
 ref:["d11e4"], doc:"dynamodb"},

{id:"q11-032", day:11, dom:4, tags:["DynamoDB"],
 q:"A social media company keeps 5 years of user posts in a DynamoDB table so users can scroll back through old content. Posts older than a few months are rarely read, and cost analysis shows that storage makes up most of the table's bill while throughput costs are small. The application must still read any post with single-digit millisecond latency using the same API calls. Which change is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Change the table class to DynamoDB Standard-Infrequent Access.",
    "Export older posts to S3 Glacier Deep Archive and delete them from the table.",
    "Purchase DynamoDB reserved capacity for the table's provisioned throughput.",
    "Switch the table from provisioned capacity to on-demand capacity mode."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. The Standard-IA table class cuts the storage price substantially in exchange for higher throughput prices, which pays off for storage-dominated tables, and reads keep the same API and latency.",
    "Archived posts could no longer be read through DynamoDB in milliseconds; they would need a restore taking up to 12 hours or more.",
    "Reserved capacity discounts throughput, which is only a small part of this table's bill, and leaves storage cost unchanged.",
    "The capacity mode changes how throughput is billed, not the price of storage, which is where most of this table's cost is."],
 ref:["d11e4"], doc:"dynamodb"},

{id:"q11-033", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Caching","RDS"],
 q:"An online retailer's product catalog runs on an Amazon RDS for MySQL db.r6g.4xlarge instance with two read replicas. More than 90% of queries are repeated product-detail and category lookups, and the business accepts product data being up to 5 minutes stale. CPU is high on all three instances, and the team plans to add two more read replicas before the holiday season. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Add the two planned read replicas of the same instance class before the season starts.",
    "Add an Amazon ElastiCache cluster with cache-aside reads and a 5-minute TTL on cached items.",
    "Scale the primary instance and both replicas up to the db.r6g.8xlarge instance class.",
    "Migrate the catalog to Amazon Aurora MySQL and add Aurora Replicas for the read traffic."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Each replica is a full DB instance billed every hour, spent recomputing the same queries; a cache serves repeated lookups far more cheaply.",
    "Correct. A cache serves the repeated lookups from memory and takes most reads off the database, so the new replicas aren't needed and existing ones may be removed or shrunk; the 5-minute TTL matches the allowed staleness.",
    "Doubling the instance class roughly doubles the cost of all three instances just to keep answering the same repeated queries.",
    "Migration adds effort, and Aurora Replicas are still billed DB instances that would keep re-running the same repeated queries."],
 ref:["d11e4","d10c2","d3c4"], doc:"elasticache"},

{id:"q11-034", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Redshift"],
 q:"A marketing analytics team runs a provisioned Amazon Redshift RA3 cluster 24/7. Analysts send queries in short, unpredictable bursts a few times a day, and the cluster is idle more than 80% of the time. The team wants to keep its Redshift SQL, tables, and BI tools, and to stop paying for idle time without having to predict when queries will arrive. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Schedule the provisioned cluster to pause outside business hours and resume each morning.",
    "Purchase reserved nodes for the provisioned cluster with a 1-year or 3-year term.",
    "Migrate the data warehouse to an Amazon Redshift Serverless workgroup.",
    "Enable concurrency scaling on the provisioned cluster to handle the query bursts."],
 a:[2],
 x:["A paused cluster cannot run queries, and because bursts arrive at unpredictable times during the day, the cluster would still have to run, and bill, through the idle daytime hours.",
    "Reserved nodes lower the hourly price but still bill every hour of the term, including the 80% of the time the cluster sits idle.",
    "Correct. Redshift Serverless bills for compute (RPU-hours) only while queries run and scales automatically, so idle time costs nothing for compute, while Redshift SQL and BI tools keep working.",
    "Concurrency scaling adds temporary capacity for bursts of concurrent queries; it does nothing about the main cluster billing 24/7 while idle."],
 ref:["d11e4","d10c3"], doc:"redshift"},

{id:"q11-035", day:11, dom:4, tags:["AWS Backup","RDS"],
 q:"A company's Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL databases keep automated backups for 35 days, and an engineer also takes a manual snapshot of each database every day that is never deleted. Snapshot storage is now a significant cost. Operations needs point-in-time recovery for the last 7 days, and compliance requires one backup per month to be kept for 1 year. Which solution meets these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
 o:["Set automated retention to 7 days, delete the manual snapshots, and use an AWS Backup plan that keeps a monthly backup for 12 months.",
    "Keep the 35-day automated backups, export each manual snapshot to Amazon S3, and transition the exported files to S3 Glacier Deep Archive.",
    "Keep the current backups and copy every snapshot to a second Region so that the oldest copies can be deleted from the first Region.",
    "Set automated backup retention to 365 days and stop taking manual snapshots, so that a single mechanism covers both requirements."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. A 7-day retention window gives the required point-in-time recovery, and the AWS Backup plan creates monthly backups and deletes each one automatically after a year, so nothing accumulates.",
    "Snapshot exports are Parquet files meant for analytics and cannot be restored as a DB instance, so they don't satisfy the backup requirement, and the over-retention remains.",
    "Cross-Region copies add inter-Region data transfer and a second copy of storage, increasing cost, and the never-deleted snapshots remain.",
    "RDS automated backups can be retained for at most 35 days, so a 365-day retention period is not possible."],
 ref:["d11e4"], doc:"backup"},

{id:"q11-036", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Aurora"],
 q:"A gaming company's Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL cluster handles a write-intensive leaderboard workload. Cost reports show that I/O charges make up more than half of the cluster's monthly Aurora bill, and the workload is expected to stay at this level. The company wants to reduce cost without changing the application or the database engine. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Add Aurora Replicas so that the I/O is spread across more DB instances.",
    "Move the cluster to Aurora serverless instances on Aurora Standard storage.",
    "Scale the writer to a larger instance class so more data fits in memory.",
    "Change the cluster's storage configuration to Aurora I/O-Optimized."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Replicas share the same cluster storage, so they do not reduce the write I/O being billed, and each one adds instance charges.",
    "With Aurora Standard storage, Aurora serverless (formerly Aurora Serverless v2) still bills every I/O request, and a steady heavy workload gains nothing from capacity scaling.",
    "More memory can reduce some read I/O, but writes must still go to storage and be billed, and the larger instance costs more.",
    "Correct. I/O-Optimized has no per-request I/O charges in exchange for higher instance and storage prices, which lowers total cost when I/O is a large share of the Aurora bill (AWS says 25% or more of Aurora spend)."],
 ref:["d11e4"], doc:"aurora"},

{id:"q11-037", day:11, dom:4, tags:["VPC endpoints & PrivateLink","VPC peering & Transit Gateway"],
 q:"A company has 60 VPCs in one Region connected through a transit gateway. Each VPC has its own interface VPC endpoints in two Availability Zones for the same eight AWS services, such as Secrets Manager, SQS, and CloudWatch Logs, and the hourly endpoint charges have become a large cost. Workloads must keep reaching these services privately by using the services' default DNS names. Which combination of actions will reduce cost the MOST? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Create the interface endpoints once in a shared services VPC attached to the transit gateway.",
    "Replace the interface endpoints with gateway VPC endpoints for all eight services in each VPC.",
    "Remove all endpoints and send the service traffic through a NAT gateway in every VPC.",
    "Associate private hosted zones that resolve the service names to the central endpoints with each VPC.",
    "Keep the endpoints in every VPC but place them in only one Availability Zone each."],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["Centralizing replaces 60 copies of each endpoint with one set in a shared VPC that every VPC reaches through the transit gateway, which removes most of the hourly endpoint charges.",
    "Gateway endpoints exist only for Amazon S3 and DynamoDB, not for services such as Secrets Manager, SQS, or CloudWatch Logs.",
    "NAT gateways add hourly and per-GB processing charges in every VPC and send the traffic to the services' public endpoints, so access is no longer private.",
    "With private DNS turned off on the central endpoints, private hosted zones for the service names, associated with every VPC, let workloads keep the default DNS names while resolving to the central endpoints.",
    "Single-AZ endpoints only halve the hourly charge and make each VPC's private access depend on one AZ; centralizing saves far more."],
 ref:["d11e5","d2c3","d2c2"], doc:"endpoints"},

{id:"q11-038", day:11, dom:4, tags:["VPC endpoints & PrivateLink","Containers"],
 q:"A company runs hundreds of Amazon ECS tasks on AWS Fargate in private subnets. The service scales out often, and every new task pulls a large container image from Amazon ECR in the same Region. All outbound traffic leaves through NAT gateways, and image pulls cause most of the NAT gateway data processing charges. Security policy requires the tasks to stay in private subnets. Which solution reduces cost the MOST?",
 o:["Move the tasks to public subnets and assign them public IP addresses to bypass the NAT gateways.",
    "Add a NAT gateway in each AZ so that every task always uses a NAT gateway in its own AZ.",
    "Create an S3 gateway endpoint plus ECR interface endpoints for the API and Docker registry.",
    "Create ECR interface endpoints for the API and Docker registry, without any S3 endpoint."],
 a:[2],
 x:["This breaks the policy that tasks must stay in private subnets and adds public IPv4 address charges.",
    "This avoids cross-AZ traffic, but every gigabyte of image data still incurs NAT gateway data processing charges, and each extra NAT gateway adds hourly charges.",
    "Correct. ECR stores image layers in S3, so the free S3 gateway endpoint carries the bulk of the pull traffic, while the ECR endpoints carry the API and registry calls privately, keeping image pulls off the NAT gateways.",
    "ECR serves image layers from Amazon S3, so without an S3 endpoint the large layer downloads would still go through the NAT gateways."],
 ref:["d11e5","d2c3","d8b1"], doc:"endpoints"},

{id:"q11-039", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Data transfer costs"],
 q:"A company's web servers and a self-managed message broker run on EC2 instances in the same VPC and the same AZ. The web servers connect to the broker by using the broker's Elastic IP address, and the monthly bill shows significant data transfer charges for this traffic. Which change will remove these data transfer charges with the LEAST effort?",
 o:["Create a VPC peering connection and route the broker traffic through it.",
    "Configure the web servers to connect to the broker's private IP address.",
    "Route the broker traffic through an AWS Transit Gateway attachment.",
    "Move the broker to another AZ and connect to it by its private IP address."],
 a:[1],
 x:["The instances are already in the same VPC, so peering doesn't apply; the charge comes from using the public address, not from the path between VPCs.",
    "Correct. Traffic between instances in the same AZ over private IP addresses is free, while traffic sent to a public or Elastic IP address is billed at data transfer rates even within the same AZ.",
    "Transit Gateway adds attachment-hour and per-GB processing charges, so it would increase the cost of this traffic.",
    "Traffic between AZs is billed per GB in each direction, so moving the broker would replace one data transfer charge with another."],
 ref:["d11e5","d11c3"], doc:"vpc"},

{id:"q11-040", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Data transfer costs","Regions & AZs"],
 q:"A genomics company runs a nightly distributed analysis job on 60 EC2 instances spread across three AZs. During each run the nodes exchange tens of terabytes of intermediate data with each other, and inter-AZ data transfer has become a large part of the bill. The job reads input from and writes results to Amazon S3, and a failed run can simply be rerun later; the job has no high availability requirement. Which change is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Launch all of the job's instances in one AZ, such as in a cluster placement group.",
    "Launch the instances in a spread placement group that covers all three of the AZs.",
    "Send the node-to-node traffic through a Transit Gateway to consolidate the transfer.",
    "Enable enhanced networking on all instances to move the shuffle data faster."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. Data transfer between instances in the same AZ over private IPs is free, and the job can tolerate an AZ outage by rerunning, so a single AZ removes the inter-AZ charges (a cluster placement group also improves network performance).",
    "A spread placement group places instances on distinct hardware and can span AZs, so node-to-node traffic would still cross AZs and be billed.",
    "A transit gateway connects VPCs and on-premises networks and charges for each gigabyte it processes; it would add cost without making the nodes any less spread across three AZs.",
    "Enhanced networking increases bandwidth and lowers latency, but inter-AZ traffic is billed the same no matter how fast it moves."],
 ref:["d11e5","d11c3","d1c6"], doc:"regions"},

{id:"q11-041", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Data transfer costs","S3 features"],
 q:"A media company uses S3 Cross-Region Replication to copy an entire 2 PB bucket to a second Region for disaster recovery. Only the finished masters under the <code>masters/</code> prefix are business-critical, and they must be readable immediately in the second Region during a disaster. The rest of the bucket holds intermediate render files that can be regenerated. Replication transfer and storage in the second Region have become a major cost. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Change the replication rule from Cross-Region Replication to Same-Region Replication instead.",
    "Keep replicating everything, but store all of the replicas in S3 Glacier Deep Archive.",
    "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the source bucket so that replication traffic costs less.",
    "Limit the replication rule to the <code>masters/</code> prefix and delete the other replicas."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Same-Region Replication keeps the copy in the same Region, so it no longer protects against a Regional disaster.",
    "Deep Archive restores take up to 12 hours or more, so masters would not be readable immediately, and inter-Region transfer for regenerable files would still be paid.",
    "Transfer Acceleration speeds up client transfers over long distances for an added fee; it does not reduce replication or inter-Region transfer charges.",
    "Correct. Replication rules can filter by prefix or tag, so only the critical masters incur inter-Region transfer and second-Region storage, while the regenerable files stay in one Region."],
 ref:["d11e5"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q11-042", day:11, dom:4, tags:["CloudFront","Data transfer costs"],
 q:"A software company distributes large installer files from an S3 bucket in one Region to customers around the world. Data transfer out to the internet from S3 is now the company's largest AWS cost, and most downloads are of the same few current releases. Which solution reduces cost the MOST?",
 o:["Serve the files through an Amazon CloudFront distribution that uses the bucket as its origin.",
    "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and give customers the accelerated endpoint.",
    "Put AWS Global Accelerator in front of the bucket to route customers over the AWS network.",
    "Replicate the bucket to three more Regions and route customers with latency-based DNS records."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. Transfer from S3 to CloudFront is free, CloudFront's data transfer out rates are generally lower than S3's, and popular releases are served from edge caches near customers.",
    "Transfer Acceleration adds a per-GB fee on top of normal data transfer and is designed to speed up long-distance transfers, especially uploads; it doesn't cache content.",
    "Global Accelerator endpoints can only be load balancers, EC2 instances, or Elastic IP addresses, not S3 buckets, and it proxies packets instead of caching content; it adds hourly and data transfer premium charges rather than reducing egress cost.",
    "Replication adds storage in three more Regions plus inter-Region transfer, and customers would still download directly from S3 at internet egress rates."],
 ref:["d11e5"], doc:"cloudfront"},

{id:"q11-043", day:11, dom:4, tags:["VPC peering & Transit Gateway","Data transfer costs"],
 q:"A company has two VPCs in the same Region: one runs a data ingestion service and the other runs analytics clusters. The VPCs exchange about 200 TB per month, and no other networks need to reach either VPC. The VPCs are currently connected through a Transit Gateway, and the company wants to lower networking costs without reducing throughput. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Run software VPN appliances on EC2 instances in each VPC and tunnel the traffic between them.",
    "Replace the Transit Gateway attachments with a VPC peering connection between the two VPCs.",
    "Expose the analytics clusters to the ingestion VPC through AWS PrivateLink endpoint services.",
    "Assign public IP addresses and send the traffic between the VPCs over their internet gateways."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Appliance instances add hourly cost and management, and tunnel throughput is limited, so moving 200 TB a month this way is harder and costlier than peering.",
    "Correct. VPC peering has no hourly or per-GB processing charge, only normal data transfer (free within the same AZ), whereas Transit Gateway bills per attachment hour and per GB processed. With just two VPCs, peering's lack of transitive routing doesn't matter.",
    "PrivateLink bills per endpoint hour and per GB processed, and it exposes a specific service rather than providing general two-way connectivity between the VPCs.",
    "Public IP traffic adds public IPv4 address charges and data transfer charges, and it exposes the systems to the internet."],
 ref:["d11e5","d2c2"], doc:"tgw"},

{id:"q11-044", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Hybrid connectivity","Data transfer costs"],
 q:"A media company's on-premises render farm downloads about 400 TB of assets from Amazon S3 every month, spread evenly through the month, over Site-to-Site VPN connections across the internet. Data transfer out charges are high and throughput is inconsistent. The company expects this pattern to continue for several years. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Add more VPN tunnels and use equal-cost multipath routing on a Transit Gateway.",
    "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration and download the assets through its endpoint.",
    "Establish an AWS Direct Connect connection and download the assets over it.",
    "Create an S3 interface endpoint in the VPC and reach it through the existing VPN."],
 a:[2],
 x:["More tunnels can raise aggregate throughput, but data still leaves AWS at internet data transfer out rates, and each VPN connection adds hourly charges.",
    "Transfer Acceleration adds a per-GB fee on top of normal data transfer out, so it raises the cost of every download.",
    "Correct. Data transfer out over Direct Connect is billed at lower rates than internet egress, and a dedicated connection gives consistent throughput, so steady high-volume transfers soon cover the port-hour charges.",
    "Traffic over the VPN still leaves AWS at normal internet data transfer out rates, and the interface endpoint adds its own hourly and per-GB charges."],
 ref:["d11e5"], doc:"dx"},

{id:"q11-045", day:11, dom:4, tags:["S3 features","Data transfer costs"],
 q:"A climate research institute publishes a 500 TB dataset in an Amazon S3 bucket that universities and companies download for their own analysis. The download traffic now costs more than the institute's grant budget can cover. All of the organizations that download the data have their own AWS accounts. The institute wants those organizations, not itself, to pay for the requests and data transfer. Which solution meets this requirement?",
 o:["Serve the dataset through an Amazon CloudFront distribution with the bucket as origin.",
    "Enable Requester Pays on the bucket and require requesters to authenticate.",
    "Transition the dataset to S3 Standard-IA to lower the cost of each download.",
    "Give each organization presigned URLs that expire after a set period of time."],
 a:[1],
 x:["CloudFront might lower the per-GB rate, but the institute, as the distribution owner, would still pay for all of the data transfer.",
    "Correct. With Requester Pays, the requester's AWS account is billed for the requests and data downloaded, while the owner pays only for storage; requests must be authenticated and acknowledge the charge.",
    "Standard-IA adds a per-GB retrieval fee to every download, and the institute would still pay all of the transfer charges.",
    "Presigned URLs grant temporary access, but the requests and data transfer are still billed to the bucket owner."],
 ref:["d11e5"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q11-046", day:11, dom:4, tags:["CloudFront"],
 q:"A European e-learning company streams video lessons through Amazon CloudFront. Nearly all students are in Europe and North America, and the few students elsewhere can accept higher latency. The company wants to lower its CloudFront costs without moving its origin or changing the application. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Disable caching on the distribution so that requests are served from the origin.",
    "Replace the CloudFront distribution with an AWS Global Accelerator accelerator.",
    "Enable CloudFront Origin Shield in the AWS Region that is closest to the origin.",
    "Switch the distribution to Price Class 100 to use only lower-cost edge locations."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Without caching, every request goes back to the origin, raising origin load and cost, and viewers are still billed as CloudFront data transfer.",
    "Global Accelerator proxies packets to the origin instead of caching content, so every video byte would come from the origin, and it adds hourly and data transfer premium charges.",
    "Origin Shield adds a caching layer that reduces load on the origin and is billed per request; it does not lower CloudFront's data transfer out charges.",
    "Correct. Price Class 100 serves the distribution only from the lowest-cost edge locations (North America, Europe, Israel, and Türkiye), which covers these students; viewers elsewhere are served from those locations with higher latency."],
 ref:["d11e5"], doc:"cloudfront"},

{id:"q11-047", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Hybrid connectivity"],
 q:"A retail chain must connect a small regional office to a VPC so staff can reach an internal inventory application. Traffic is only a few gigabytes per day, the office already has reliable broadband internet, and encrypted connectivity must be working within a week. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Set up an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection from the office router to the VPC.",
    "Order a 1 Gbps AWS Direct Connect dedicated connection for the office.",
    "Order a Direct Connect hosted connection for the office through an AWS Partner.",
    "Create an AWS Client VPN endpoint and install the VPN client on each office PC."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Correct. Site-to-Site VPN runs encrypted IPsec tunnels over the existing internet connection, can be set up in hours, and costs little for low traffic volumes.",
    "A dedicated connection usually takes weeks to provision, bills port hours whether used or not, and is not encrypted by default, which is overkill for a few gigabytes a day.",
    "Hosted connections still take time to provision through a partner, add port and circuit charges, and are not encrypted by default.",
    "Client VPN bills per endpoint association hour and per connection hour, and a client on every PC is meant for remote users, not for linking a whole office."],
 ref:["d11e5"], doc:"vpn"},

{id:"q11-048", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Cost management tools"],
 q:"A company runs workloads for 12 product teams in one shared AWS account and cannot move to separate accounts this year. Finance wants a monthly cost breakdown per team and wants to view each team's spending trend in AWS Cost Explorer. Which actions should a solutions architect take? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Apply a consistent team tag to all resources, requiring it in the infrastructure as code templates.",
    "Create a separate VPC for each team so that network resources can be billed to each team separately.",
    "Create an AWS CloudTrail trail to record which team's users create each resource in the account.",
    "Activate the team tag as a user-defined cost allocation tag in the Billing and Cost Management console.",
    "Turn on AWS Cost Anomaly Detection so that each team's costs are grouped automatically by usage."],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["Correct. Costs can be split by team only if resources carry the tag consistently; requiring it in templates makes every new resource tagged at creation.",
    "VPCs are network boundaries, not billing boundaries; costs are not reported per VPC, and many resources don't live in a VPC at all.",
    "CloudTrail records API activity for auditing; it does not attribute costs to teams in billing data or Cost Explorer.",
    "Correct. User-defined tags appear in Cost Explorer and the Cost and Usage Report only after they are activated as cost allocation tags; then costs can be grouped and filtered by team.",
    "Cost Anomaly Detection alerts on unusual spending; it does not attribute or group costs by team."],
 ref:["d11e2"], doc:"costexplorer"},

{id:"q11-049", day:11, dom:4, tags:["EFS"],
 q:"A software company's development and test environments run entirely in a single Availability Zone. Their build servers share artifacts and test datasets on a Regional Amazon EFS file system that stores data in the EFS Standard storage class. The files are read and written constantly during builds, and all of them can be regenerated from source if lost. The company wants to lower the storage cost of this file system. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Add a lifecycle policy that moves files to EFS Infrequent Access after 1 day unused.",
    "Switch the file system to Provisioned throughput mode sized for the build peaks.",
    "Move the data to a new EFS One Zone file system in that same Availability Zone.",
    "Replace the file system with gp3 EBS volumes attached to each build server."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Lifecycle management moves only files that have not been accessed for the set period; these files are used constantly, so few would move, and those that did would add Infrequent Access read charges on their next use.",
    "Provisioned throughput adds a throughput charge and does nothing to reduce storage cost.",
    "A One Zone file system keeps data in a single AZ at a lower storage price than a Regional one, which suits single-AZ dev and test data that can be regenerated.",
    "Separate EBS volumes cannot be shared by all build servers the way the file system is, so the builds would break."],
 ref:["d11e2","d3c1"], doc:"efs"},

{id:"q11-050", day:11, dom:4, tags:["Cost management tools","Data transfer costs"],
 q:"A company's networking costs have grown sharply. The FinOps team needs hourly, resource-level line items that show which resources incur NAT gateway data processing, inter-AZ, and inter-Region data transfer charges. The team wants to query 12 months of this data with SQL and join it with internal ownership data. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Filter AWS Cost Explorer by usage type and download the monthly reports.",
    "Deliver the Cost and Usage Report to Amazon S3 and query it with Amazon Athena.",
    "Enable VPC Flow Logs and analyze them with CloudWatch Logs Insights queries.",
    "Create AWS Budgets usage budgets for each data transfer usage type."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Cost Explorer's hourly data comes without resource-level detail for anything except EC2 instances, and it is a visual tool, not a dataset of line items to join with other tables in SQL.",
    "Correct. The Cost and Usage Report (now delivered through AWS Data Exports) is the most detailed billing data, with hourly line items and resource IDs, and Athena can query it in S3 with SQL alongside other tables.",
    "Flow logs show traffic between IP addresses, not what that traffic costs, so they cannot produce the billing line items the team needs.",
    "Usage budgets alert when usage crosses a threshold; they don't provide line-item data to query."],
 ref:["d11e5","d11c3"], doc:"data-exports"}
);
